From: Vipul Cariappa <vipulcariappa@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 12:26:07 +0530
Subject: Do not inspect a nonexistent executable when initializing the dyld

initializeDyld opens the running executable as an object file to learn its
format:

    std::string exeP = GetExecutablePath();
    auto ObjF = cantFail(llvm::object::ObjectFile::createObjectFile(exeP));

Under emscripten there is no such file. createObjectFile fails on whatever
GetExecutablePath returns and cantFail turns that into an abort, so the
DynamicLibraryManager can never be constructed at all -- and the manager is
built lazily on first use, which means the abort surfaces from whichever API
happens to touch it first rather than from here.

In practice that was AddSearchPath, called during cppjit's import to register
library paths. What a reader saw was a wasm trap, "indirect call to null",
reported against a JIT'd wrapper several frames up, with nothing tying it to a
dyld that had failed to initialize. Every route into AddSearchPath died the same
way, which is what eventually gave it away: the cause had to be inside the
function rather than in how it was reached.

Name the format directly instead of deriving it from a file. Symbol resolution
under emscripten goes through its own dlopen/dlsym, so there is no native loader
to interrogate and nothing that depends on the answer.

(cherry picked from commit 4aafe17b778bad5cf0c498530af03430a7fb1ea3)
---
 lib/CppInterOp/DynamicLibraryManagerSymbol.cpp | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/lib/CppInterOp/DynamicLibraryManagerSymbol.cpp b/lib/CppInterOp/DynamicLibraryManagerSymbol.cpp
index abc77bb..18b1fea 100644
--- a/lib/CppInterOp/DynamicLibraryManagerSymbol.cpp
+++ b/lib/CppInterOp/DynamicLibraryManagerSymbol.cpp
@@ -1314,11 +1314,22 @@ void DynamicLibraryManager::initializeDyld(
   if (m_Dyld)
     delete m_Dyld;
 
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+  // A browser has no executable object file to inspect and no native dynamic
+  // loader to enumerate: createObjectFile fails on whatever GetExecutablePath
+  // returns, and cantFail turns that into an abort. The abort surfaces from
+  // whichever API first reaches the manager -- AddSearchPath, in practice --
+  // as a wasm trap with nothing to connect it back to here. Symbol resolution
+  // under emscripten goes through its own dlopen/dlsym anyway, so name the
+  // format directly and skip the inspection.
+  m_Dyld = new Dyld(*this, shouldPermanentlyIgnore, "WASM");
+#else
   std::string exeP = GetExecutablePath();
   auto ObjF = cantFail(llvm::object::ObjectFile::createObjectFile(exeP));
 
   m_Dyld = new Dyld(*this, shouldPermanentlyIgnore,
                     ObjF.getBinary()->getFileFormatName());
+#endif
 }
 
 std::string DynamicLibraryManager::searchLibrariesForSymbol(
